Summary of .NET, C#, and ASP.NET MVC/Core
🔹 What is .NET?
.NET Framework: Microsoft’s original runtime for Windows desktop/web apps.

.NET Core: Cross-platform, open-source successor (Windows, Linux, macOS).

.NET 5+ (Unified .NET): Combines .NET Framework and Core into one platform.

Provides CLR (Common Language Runtime) for execution and BCL (Base Class Library) for reusable components.

🔹 C# Language
Object-oriented, type-safe, modern language.

Features: Classes, Interfaces, Generics, LINQ, Delegates, Events, Async/Await.

Strongly integrated with .NET runtime.

🔹 ASP.NET MVC / Core
MVC Pattern: Model (data), View (UI), Controller (logic).

ASP.NET MVC: Framework for building web apps on .NET Framework.

ASP.NET Core MVC: Cross-platform, lightweight, modular, supports Razor Pages, Web API, and Blazor.

Key features: Routing, Middleware, Dependency Injection, Tag Helpers, Identity for authentication.

🔹 Why .NET is Popular
Cross-platform development.

Rich ecosystem (NuGet packages, Visual Studio tooling).

Performance improvements in .NET Core.

Enterprise-grade security and scalability.

📝 25 Interview Questions & Answers (Detailed)
1. What is the CLR in .NET?
Answer:  
The Common Language Runtime (CLR) is the execution engine of .NET. It provides:

Memory management (Garbage Collection).

Type safety.

Exception handling.

JIT compilation (IL → native code).

Security enforcement.
Think of CLR as the “brain” that runs all .NET applications.

2. Difference between .NET Framework, .NET Core, and .NET 5+?
Answer:

.NET Framework: Windows-only, legacy.

.NET Core: Cross-platform, modular, high performance.

.NET 5+: Unified platform, supports desktop, mobile, cloud, IoT.
Example: A web app built in .NET Core can run on Linux servers, unlike .NET Framework.

3. Explain Managed vs Unmanaged Code.
Answer:

Managed Code: Runs under CLR supervision (C#, VB.NET). CLR handles memory, exceptions.

Unmanaged Code: Runs directly on OS (C/C++). Developer must manage memory manually.
Example: Calling a C++ DLL from C# requires P/Invoke.

4. What is JIT Compilation?
Answer:

JIT = Just-In-Time compiler.

Converts IL (Intermediate Language) into native machine code at runtime.

Types: Pre-JIT, Econo-JIT, Normal JIT.
Benefit: Optimized execution for the target machine.

5. Explain Garbage Collection in .NET.
Answer:

GC automatically frees unused memory.

Works in generations (0,1,2) for efficiency.

Uses mark-and-sweep algorithm.
Example: Local variables in a method are collected once the method finishes.

6. What are Assemblies in .NET?
Answer:

Assemblies = compiled code units (.dll or .exe).

Contain metadata + IL code.

Two types: Private (used by one app) and Shared (GAC).
Example: Newtonsoft.Json.dll is a shared assembly used across apps.

7. Difference between Value Type and Reference Type in C#.
Answer:

Value Type: Stored in stack (int, struct). Copy by value.

Reference Type: Stored in heap (class, object). Copy by reference.
Example: int a=5; int b=a; → independent copies.
Class obj1=obj2; → both point to same memory.

8. What is Boxing and Unboxing?
Answer:

Boxing: Converting value type → object.
int x=10; object o=x;

Unboxing: Converting object → value type.
int y=(int)o;  
Performance hit occurs due to heap allocation.

9. Explain Delegates in C#.
Answer:

Delegate = type-safe function pointer.

Used for callbacks, event handling.
Example:

csharp
public delegate void Print(string msg);
Print p = Console.WriteLine;
p("Hello");
10. What are Events in C#?
Answer:

Events = wrapper around delegates.

Publisher/Subscriber model.
Example: Button click in WinForms triggers an event.

11. Explain LINQ in C#.
Answer:

Language Integrated Query.

Query collections using SQL-like syntax.
Example:

csharp
var result = from s in students
             where s.Age > 18
             select s.Name;
12. Difference between Abstract Class and Interface.
Answer:

Abstract Class: Can have fields, constructors, default implementations.

Interface: Only contracts (methods, properties).
Example: Use abstract class for base functionality, interface for multiple inheritance.

13. What is Dependency Injection in ASP.NET Core?
Answer:

DI = design pattern to decouple objects.

ASP.NET Core has built-in DI container.
Example:

csharp
services.AddScoped<IRepo, Repo>();
Controller gets IRepo injected automatically.

14. Explain Middleware in ASP.NET Core.
Answer:

Middleware = pipeline components that handle requests/responses.

Example: Authentication, Logging, Routing.
Code:

csharp
app.UseRouting();
app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseEndpoints(...);
15. Difference between MVC and Web API.
Answer:

MVC: Returns Views (HTML).

Web API: Returns data (JSON/XML).
Example: MVC for user-facing website, Web API for mobile app backend.

16. Explain Routing in ASP.NET Core MVC.
Answer:

Maps URL → Controller/Action.

Types: Conventional, Attribute routing.
Example:

csharp
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class ProductsController : Controller { }
17. What is Razor in ASP.NET Core?
Answer:

Razor = view engine for mixing C# + HTML.

Syntax: @Model.Name inside HTML.
Lightweight compared to WebForms.

18. Explain Authentication vs Authorization.
Answer:

Authentication: Verifying identity (login).

Authorization: Verifying access rights (roles/permissions).
Example: User logs in (authN), then allowed to view admin page (authZ).

19. What is ASP.NET Identity?
Answer:

Membership system for authentication/authorization.

Supports roles, claims, external logins (Google, Facebook).
Example: UserManager class manages users.

20. Explain Entity Framework Core.
Answer:

ORM for .NET.

Maps C# classes → DB tables.

Supports LINQ queries.
Example:

csharp
var users = db.Users.Where(u => u.IsActive).ToList();
21. Difference between Synchronous and Asynchronous programming.
Answer:

Sync: Blocks until task completes.

Async: Frees thread, continues later.
Example:

csharp
await db.Users.ToListAsync();
22. Explain Threading in C#.
Answer:

Thread = unit of execution.

Managed via Thread class or Task Parallel Library.
Example: Task.Run(()=>DoWork());

23. What is ViewData, ViewBag, TempData in MVC?
Answer:

ViewData: Dictionary, requires casting.

ViewBag: Dynamic wrapper around ViewData.

TempData: Stores data for one request (redirect).
Example: TempData["msg"]="Saved";

24. Explain Filters in ASP.NET MVC.
Answer:

Filters = attributes for cross-cutting concerns.

Types: Authorization, Action, Result, Exception.
Example: [Authorize] ensures only logged-in users access.

25. Explain Difference between .NET Core and .NET Framework in terms of Hosting.
Answer:

.NET Framework: Hosted on IIS.

.NET Core: Self-hosted via Kestrel, can integrate with IIS, Nginx, Apache.
Benefit: Cross-platform deployment flexibility.
